te must  and at the top of that list is your cpu  more powerful cpus can really help when  it comes to video encoding and  transcoding the more cores and the more  threads that the cpu has the easier it  will be to stream at higher resolutions  and even higher frame rates modern intel  i7s and amd ryzen 7s will give you  plenty of flexibility if you want a  higher quality streaming experience so  while your graphics card is busy  actually rendering the game your cpu is  handling things on the streaming side  the work of rendering the game that is  the act of actually creating the image  has already been done by your graphics  card it's then your cpu that takes that  rendered image and transcodes it into a  video format that can be easily sent  over the internet and displayed by your  viewers web browsers okay dimitri just  used a word three times there that he  almost certainly can't define clearly  transcode so let me help us all out with  a definition transcoding and encoding  are similar processes and you'll often  hear people just use the word encoding  for both but there is an important  difference erica eins gives a great  description of encoding over on  api.video as anything where the video  file contains data that comes directly  from a camera sensor transcoding is when  you take that already encoded file and  convert it into another format so your  pc is encoding your webcam and gameplay  feeds on the way into your streaming  software and then transcoding it on the  way out into a format that your  streaming platform servers can recognize  and push out to viewers now having a  powerful cpu will go a long way in the  world of streaming  but it isn't the only option anymore  streaming software like obs allows you  to use nvenk encoding rather than h.264  which lets your nvidia gpu do more of  the heavy lifting so if your gpu is a  sick new 3090 but your cpu is some  three-year-old i5 then nvenk might be  for you now we've talked a lot about  single pc setups but in the world of  content creation there are some who've  opted to go a different route some  streamers though actually decide to go a  step further and use a second pc to  handle all the streaming tasks using  their first rig strictly for actual  gaming  this can especially make sense if you  already have an older tower lying around  because it's a great way to stream  cpu-intensive games like starcraft or  total war without being concerned that  your gameplay or your stream quality  will suffer as a result the best part  about having a dedicated streaming pc is  that it doesn't need to be a maxed out  gaming rig with a ryzen 9 and a 30  whatever  hell you may not even need to put a  graphics card in it at all while one pc  focuses on actually playing the game the  secondary streaming pc only has to worry  about transcoding the video and sending  it off to your streaming service of  choice and since it's only focusing on  transcoding cpus like the ryzen 3 series  or intel's i3 and i5 models are  oftentimes more than enough despite the  fact that most of them are only  quad-core processors they're typically  able to pump out more than enough power  for transcoding on a basic streaming pc  since all you'll be running are programs  like xsplit or obs and some content  creators swear by these dual pc setups  as far as a pro player goes i don't  think any pro player has a rig like this  the whole point of a multi-computer  stream is so that your main computer can  focus all of its power on the game so i  have good fps it doesn't lag but what  made me switch to a 2p setup two pc  setup is when you're playing games you  want like the maximum frame rate  possible and when you have a one pc  setup the stress on both your cpu and  your gpu  is like  just enough  to have it  affect your stream and your game at the  same time so if you want to play at high  settings like  you can you can almost forget playing at  high settings if you want to stream it  like a high quality 